#!/usr/bin/env bash

lordhavemercy -f --verbose << "EOF"
The implementation of `@`, `at`, `LD`, `NHS`, and `nhs`: a new approach to the shell.

1. In bin we have _ and __ that are a sort of lazy half implemented 'cat most recent stdout of a lord command' and 'save [ibid]'.
    We need a more serious way of interacting with the most recent stdin, stdout, and stderr of all lord commands so that we can
    have the language in which to properly implement:
        1. LD (the ld.so of "Executable English" era)
            Since /lib/ld.so is sort of the "shebang" of every binary,
            our file LD is the "shebang" of all executable english.
            It's basically identical to NSH, *except* for the difference
            in behavior that results from the fact that NSH is meant to be
            run interactively, and LD is *the same language* and same conventions as NSH
            (and even optionally the same ~/.nshrc and other runtime config)
            but for the crucial difference that #!/bin/LD meant to be run
            non-interactively.
        2. NSH (New Human Shell)
        3. @ (the highest level of intelligence and reasoning that the shell provides)
EOF
